The cost of renewable energy has fallen 90% since 2010 — solar is now the cheapest electricity source in history, meaning the transition is increasingly economically attractive, not just necessary.
The 2022 US Inflation Reduction Act projects $3 trillion in clean energy investment over a decade, demonstrating that climate policy and economic growth are compatible.
Munich Re data shows economic losses from natural disasters have tripled in real terms since the 1980s, with a clear trend toward more costly events.
Health co-benefits from reduced air pollution alone could save 7 million lives per year — the WHO calls air pollution the world's largest environmental health risk.
Every $1 spent on disaster risk reduction saves $6 in avoided losses, according to the UN Office for Disaster Risk Reduction.
